-- Autopilot campaigns advance on a clock, not on somebody having a tab open.
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS
--
-- `autopilot.tsx` says it in its own header: "this environment has no server
-- cron — the runner is driven by a client heartbeat." So a campaign only moved
-- forward while the Autopilot page was open in a browser, which means every
-- overnight and weekend slot silently never posted. The route written for an
-- external scheduler, /autopilot/tick, was never called by anything.
--
-- pg_cron is already installed here and already drives
-- `compliance-expiry-reminders`. This follows that job deliberately rather than
-- inventing a second scheme: same http_post shape, same anon key row, same
-- shared-secret header, secret read from `private_cron_config` so rotating it
-- means changing one row rather than a row and an environment variable that
-- can disagree.
--
-- WHY .pending
--
-- Named `.sql.pending` like `20260923000000_schedule_on_call_escalation`, so it
-- is NOT applied by a deploy. This one schedules a job that publishes to live
-- Facebook and Instagram business pages with nobody watching, and the standing
-- rule is that backend changes are proven in a non-production project first.
-- Rename to `.sql` to arm it, deliberately.
--
-- BEFORE ARMING THIS, READ:
--
-- Autopilot campaigns support `requireApproval`, which holds each item until a
-- human approves it. Leave that ON for the first campaign: the cron will then
-- prove it is running by moving items to "ready" without anything reaching a
-- page until you press approve. Turning it off afterwards is one setting, and
-- by then you will have watched the machine work.

-- The secret the scheduler sends and the route checks. Generated here so it
-- never has to be typed anywhere, and stored in the same private table the
-- compliance secret already uses — revoked from public, anon and authenticated.
insert into private_cron_config (key, value)
values (
  'autopilot_cron_secret',
  encode(gen_random_bytes(32), 'base64')
)
on conflict (key) do nothing;

-- Unschedule first so re-running this is safe.
select cron.unschedule('autopilot-tick')
where exists (select 1 from cron.job where jobname = 'autopilot-tick');

-- Every fifteen minutes. Campaign items carry their own `dueAt` and are guarded
-- by `postedAt`, so a tick that finds nothing due does nothing and a double
-- fire cannot double-post. Fifteen minutes is close enough that a slot lands
-- near its intended time without asking the function to wake up constantly.
select cron.schedule(
  'autopilot-tick',
  '*/15 * * * *',
  $$
  select net.http_post(
    url     := 'https://plzsvzwwcdopnawtiwzm.supabase.co/functions/v1/make-server-3eae23a6/autopilot/cron-tick',
    headers := jsonb_build_object(
      'Content-Type', 'application/json',
      'Authorization', 'Bearer ' || (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'anon_key'),
      'X-Autopilot-Cron-Secret', (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'autopilot_cron_secret')
    ),
    body    := '{}'::jsonb
  );
  $$
);
